{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgg5zKot6KcAmRkev62dW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For the low-spin d⁶ complex [Co(ox)₃]³⁻, Dq ≈ 1856 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 432 cm⁻¹, with C ≈ 1946 cm⁻¹ from C = 4.5B.\n\n**Approach:** I used the standard strong-field (first-order) energies for low-spin octahedral d⁶, measured from the ¹A₁g ground state:\n\n- E(¹A₁g → ¹T₁g) = 10Dq − C\n- E(¹A₁g → ¹T₂g) = 10Dq + 16B − C\n\n**Racah B:** Subtracting the first expression from the second leaves only B:\n16B = 23 529 − 16 611 = 6 918 cm⁻¹\nB = 432.4 cm⁻¹\n\n**Racah C:** C = 4.5 × 432.4 = 1 945.7 cm⁻¹\n\n**Splitting Dq:** From the first band:\n10Dq = 16 611 + 1 945.7 = 18 556.7 cm⁻¹\nDq = 1 855.7 cm⁻¹\n\n**Results:**\n- Dq ≈ 1856 cm⁻¹ (10Dq ≈ 18 557 cm⁻¹)\n- B ≈ 432 cm⁻¹\n- C ≈ 1946 cm⁻¹\n\nThese first-order formulas leave out the mixing between states of the same symmetry from different configurations (configuration interaction). Including that mixing would shift the values slightly. The ratio B/B₀ ≈ 0.38 (B₀ ≈ 1100 cm⁻¹ for free Co³⁺) shows a strong nephelauxetic effect, meaning the ligands make the bonding noticeably more covalent.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1855.7, \"B\": 432.4, \"C\": 1945.7, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Used strong-field d6 expressions 10Dq−C and 10Dq+16B−C with C=4.5B.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":820,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":820,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e1635018-1c44-41fa-a4ec-c3b55e215075","uuid":"c60ba88c-1323-4fd0-a3bc-68909b27994a","timestamp":"2026-11-03T13:38:36.828Z","request_id":"req_011Cgg5zKT48KtD2HpbTjoq3"}
